**@Test Annotation Attributes:**

**1. priority** - Control execution order
```java
@Test(priority = 1)
public void loginTest() {
    // Runs first
}

@Test(priority = 2)
public void dashboardTest() {
    // Runs second
}
```
- Lower number = higher priority
- Default priority = 0
- Tests with same priority run alphabetically

**2. description** - Add test description
```java
@Test(description = "Verify user can login with valid credentials")
public void testValidLogin() {
    // Test code
}
```
- Appears in test reports
- Documents test purpose
- Improves readability

**3. groups** - Organize tests
```java
@Test(groups = {"smoke", "regression"})
public void criticalTest() {
    // Part of smoke and regression suites
}

@Test(groups = {"regression"})
public void detailedTest() {
    // Only in regression suite
}
```
- Run specific groups: `mvn test -Dgroups=smoke`
- Multiple groups per test
- Easy selective execution

**4. dependsOnMethods** - Define dependencies
```java
@Test(priority = 1)
public void login() {
    // Login test
}

@Test(priority = 2, dependsOnMethods = {"login"})
public void addToCart() {
    // Skipped if login fails
}
```
- Creates test dependency chain
- Dependent test skipped if dependency fails
- Multiple dependencies allowed

**5. enabled** - Enable/disable tests
```java
@Test(enabled = false)
public void workInProgressTest() {
    // This test won't run
}

@Test(enabled = true)  // Default
public void activeTest() {
    // This test will run
}
```
- Temporarily disable tests
- Better than commenting out
- Still visible in reports as skipped

**6. timeOut** - Maximum execution time
```java
@Test(timeOut = 5000)  // 5 seconds
public void performanceTest() {
    // Fails if takes longer than 5 seconds
}
```
- In milliseconds
- Fails test if exceeded
- Useful for performance testing

**7. expectedExceptions** - Expect exceptions
```java
@Test(expectedExceptions = ArithmeticException.class)
public void divisionTest() {
    int result = 10 / 0;  // Expected to throw exception
}
```
- Test passes if exception is thrown
- Test fails if no exception
- Useful for negative testing

**8. invocationCount** - Repeat tests
```java
@Test(invocationCount = 3)
public void stabilityTest() {
    // Runs 3 times
}
```
- Runs test multiple times
- Good for stability testing
- Each run counted separately

**9. alwaysRun** - Run even if dependencies fail
```java
@Test(alwaysRun = true, dependsOnMethods = {"setup"})
public void cleanup() {
    // Runs even if setup fails
}
```
- Useful for cleanup methods
- Ensures cleanup always happens

